On Tue, Apr 9, 2013 at 3:16 PM, selpa'i <seladwa@gmx.de> wrote:

> Just to mention an option for this particular case:
>
> lo [litki] djacu cu dunja lo kelvo be li 273
>
> lo [sligu] djacu cu runme lo kelvo be li 273

These don't really play nice with my ideas about quantities; {lo kelvo be
li 273} is the water/ice itself in my model, not some ethereal
"temperature". (Getting at "temperature" that way under this model is
something like {lo si'o kelvo joi li 273}, but is never actually required.)
In either case, this just makes the concept primitive and embeds it in
opaque terms, which obfuscates the "decrease"/"increase" part that you
would need to explain the general principle to someone who doesn't
understand it already. (Specifically, objects melt when heated and freeze
when cooled, they do not simply melt/freeze at a certain
temperature/pressure.)

> How about:
>
> zenba [co'a ko'a] lo ka ma kau ni glare kei lo se sumji be li 273 [bei lo
> me ko'a moi]
>
> mu'o mi'e la selpa'i

Valid but unnecessarily clumsy; you're being forced to describe the
subtraction when the actual content of the subtraction is essentially
superfluous. Also, I'd suggest:
zenba lo ka ce'u kelvo ma kau kei lo se sumji be li 273
if we were going to use that structure anyway. Let {ni} be weird/vague, use
{ma kau} and numbers when you have precise things in mind.

mi'e la latro'a mu'o

